Description: Do not abort when a subscriber goes away while connecting
 If a client unsubscribes while the initial event notification is still
 connecting to its callback URL, upnpevents_removeSubscriber() clears
 notify->sub, and upnp_event_prepare() then hits assert(obj->sub) and
 aborts the whole daemon.  Any host on the LAN can trigger this with a
 SUBSCRIBE to an unresponsive callback followed by an UNSUBSCRIBE.
 Drop such notifications instead.
Author: Alexander GQ Gerasiov <gq@debian.org>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/1144655
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-08
---
diff --git a/upnpevents.c b/upnpevents.c
index 1e44ffe..23033a0 100644
--- a/upnpevents.c
+++ b/upnpevents.c
@@ -408,6 +408,14 @@ upnp_event_process_notify(struct event *ev)
 	switch(obj->state) {
 	case EConnecting:
 		/* now connected or failed to connect */
+		if (!obj->sub) {
+			/* The subscriber was removed (UNSUBSCRIBE) while
+			 * we were connecting: drop the notification. */
+			DPRINTF(E_DEBUG, L_HTTP, "upnp_event_process_notify: subscriber gone, dropping notify\n");
+			obj->state = EFinished;
+			event_module.del(&obj->ev, EV_FLAG_CLOSING);
+			break;
+		}
 		upnp_event_prepare(obj);
 		upnp_event_send(obj);
 		break;
